Play one sound at a time, like Windows' sndPlaySound().

The original game played its sounds with sndPlaySound(), which stops
the sound being played before starting the next one, and the scripts
rely on that: their priorities ask whether "the" sound has finished.
The engine started each sound on a new SDL channel without stopping
the previous one, so sounds piled up on the 4 channels until the next
one failed with "Sound playing on channel -1 (max 4)", leaking the
loaded sound. Its id -1 then made the priority test check every
channel, holding back sounds while any one was still playing. Stop the
previous sound first, and free a sound that cannot be played.

--- a/win16.cpp
+++ b/win16.cpp
@@ -20,6 +20,8 @@
 			const char *fileName = (const char *)data;
 			File *f = _fs.openFile(fileName, false);
 			if (f) {
+				// Like Windows, play one sound at a time: stop the previous one
+				_mixer->stopSound(_mixerSoundId);
 				_mixer->playSound(f, &_mixerSoundId);
 				_fs.closeFile(f);
 			} else {
